<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Landed since your last approval</h2>
<p>You read item 27 at 11:41 UTC on 6 November 2026, so it is not on the page.</p>
<p>Two items, 28 and 29, changes to the coordinator skill that you have not read. Each shows its change against the text before it.</p>
<p>In the item, the words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on the item, or say so in chat. The skill writer then restores the old words.</p>

<section id="l28" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 28 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;Quiet times&rdquo; and &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;</span>Your standing order on branches</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that came in. No words went. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;Talking to the curator&rdquo;, &ldquo;Quiet times&rdquo;, a new bullet, line 186</p>
<ul><li><span class="add">The curator deletes the branches on GitHub, when they get to them. The permission check also refuses the deletion from this session. Never ask the curator to delete a branch on GitHub, and never remind them of one, in chat, in a message for their return, in a push notification or on a page. Drop a branch&rsquo;s deletion from every ask, close and list that the record holds. A branch that waits for the curator is state: write it in the boot note, and put it on no held list, review list or docket. If the curator asks about branches, answer from the tree.</span></li></ul>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The record: what goes where&rdquo;, the bullet on pushing, line 235, one sentence added</p>
<ul><li>Push after every commit, in the order of <code>fortress-repo</code>&rsquo;s committing part. <span class="add">Do not try to delete a branch on GitHub: the permission check refuses it, and the curator deletes it.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The skill had no rule on branches. The session&rsquo;s permission check refuses every deletion of a branch on GitHub from this container. Even so, the coordinator asked you twice to delete the scratch branch <code>tmp-push-probe</code>, the second time just before 12:00 UTC on 6 November. You answered that this was to have been a standing order: nobody tries such a deletion, and nobody asks or reminds you, since you delete the branches on GitHub when you get to them. The text now says so. It also drops a branch&rsquo;s deletion from every ask and list in the record, keeps a branch that waits for you as state in the boot note only, and has the coordinator answer from the tree if you ask about branches.</p>
<p>The cold read of the first text found that it also forbade deleting branches here in the container. The record-landing workflow makes such local deletions, and they work. The fixed text covers only the branches on GitHub.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l29" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 29 &middot; SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo; and &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo;</span>After a light boot, the first batch landing reads FACTS and POSITIONS whole</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; struck through, the words that went; underlined, the words that came in. In the skill's words, &ldquo;you&rdquo; is the coordinator.</span>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;, line 251</p>
<p><strong>A light boot.</strong> When the curator asks for a light boot, take steps 0 and 3 to 8, and skip steps 1 and 2: FACTS, POSITIONS and INDEX stay unread. Before you state a fact or a decision, or put anything to the curator, search the record with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>. <span class="add">INDEX stays unread whole: search it with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>. At the first batch landing after that boot, before step 1 of <code>references/batch.md</code>, &ldquo;The landing&rdquo;, read <code>FACTS.md</code> and <code>POSITIONS.md</code> whole. Naming each decision that landed and putting the items on the review list (its step 2), and planning the batch after it, rest on the whole record. Read <code>FACTS.md</code> in pages, as step 1 says. After the record-landing workflow of its step 4 ends, read what it changed in FACTS and POSITIONS: with the whole read, this gives you the current record for the planning. A context that has read the two files whole holds them, so a later landing in the same context reads only what the landing changed, as after a full boot.</span> The full boot<span class="add">, steps 0 to 8,</span> is the default. The curator's word for a light boot replaces the hook's list of files after a compaction.</p>
<p class="where-in">SKILL.md, &ldquo;The boot&rdquo;, &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo;, line 259</p>
<p><strong>At a landing.</strong> A landing's work never waits for a compaction or a boot, and you ask the curator nothing about the boot. <span class="cut">At a landing,</span> <span class="add">After a full boot,</span> read <span class="add">at a landing</span> what the landing changed in the record, not FACTS and POSITIONS whole. <span class="add">After a light boot, read FACTS and POSITIONS whole at the first batch landing after that boot, and at a later landing in the same context only what the landing changed (&ldquo;A light boot&rdquo;).</span> Read the whole record when you plan a batch. <span class="add">After a light boot, the whole read at the landing serves the planning of the batch after it, and INDEX stays searched with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>.</span></p>
<p class="where-in">references/sources.md, a new section, line 388, its heading</p>
<p><span class="add"><strong>&ldquo;Landing should trigger full load&rdquo;: the first batch landing after a light boot, 2026-11-07</strong></span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> After a light boot, FACTS and POSITIONS stayed unread, and the skill said nothing more: at a landing the coordinator read only what the landing changed. So the landing of a batch, where the coordinator names the decisions that landed and plans the batch after it, rested on searches of the record alone. You asked, in chat just before 06:00 UTC on 7 November, that a landing trigger a full read of POSITIONS and FACTS after a light boot. The text now says that the first batch landing after a light boot reads the two files whole, before the landing's first step, and then what the record-landing workflow changed in them. A later landing in the same context reads only what it changed, since that context already holds the two files. INDEX stays unread whole and is searched with <code>facts-extract.sh 'index:WORDS'</code>.</p>
<p>You said &ldquo;landing&rdquo;; the coordinator settled that only the first landing after the boot reads the files whole. The cold read of the first text found the read&rsquo;s place in the landing and its reason vague, &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; still saying that a landing never reads the files whole, and no word on how INDEX is searched. The fixed text places the read before the landing&rsquo;s first step, ties it to the naming of decisions and the planning, brings &ldquo;At a landing&rdquo; in line, and names the search.</p></div>
</section>

